\section{Conclusion}
This work established a reproducible grey-box modeling framework designed to characterize motor terminal impedance across a six-decade frequency spectrum, extending from $10^2$ Hz to the ultra-high-frequency (UHF) regime of $10^8$ Hz. Structural failure analysis identified that the limitations of conventional grey-box formulations in the $10^7$–$10^8$ Hz range originate from a fundamental inversion of branch dominance, where critical inductive components are masked by low-impedance capacitive bypass paths. By introducing a port-side $L_{ad}–C_{ad}–R_{ad}$ resonant cell—motivated by measurement boundary dynamics—the proposed framework successfully restores the structural degrees of freedom necessary to recover anomalous phase upward turns and late-frequency magnitude re-ascents.

The proposed identification pipeline, which integrates automated spectral feature extraction with a hybrid global-to-local optimization strategy, demonstrated high numerical fidelity ($RMSE_{\text{raw}} = 130.872$) and exceptional cross-seed robustness. Ablation studies and sensitivity analyses confirmed that the synergy between analytical initialization, frequency-dependent residual weighting, and separate real/imaginary scaling is essential for maintaining parameter stability and identifiability. Furthermore, comparative benchmarks against Bayesian No-U-Turn Sampling (NUTS) verified that the remaining UHF residuals are attributable to the stochastic, high-order irregularities of the measurement environment rather than optimization deficiencies, thus validating the structural sufficiency of the augmented model.

Ultimately, the realized framework provides a physically consistent and computationally efficient bridge for system-level electromagnetic interference (EMI) prediction and transient overvoltage analysis. By decoupling boundary-related parasitic effects from the internal motor core parameters, the model preserves mid-band interpretability while ensuring reliable performance at the ultra-high-frequency limits demanded by modern fast-switching drive systems.

On this basis, the present work makes three main contributions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item It establishes a grey-box modeling framework capable of addressing anomalous motor impedance behavior in the $10^7$--$10^8$ Hz range.
    \item It proposes a more reproducible parameter identification pipeline that reduces reliance on difficult-to-measure quantities and specialized experimental procedures.
    \item It demonstrates that the ultra-high-frequency improvement arises primarily from structural augmentation rather than from the use of stronger optimizers alone.
\end{itemize}\section{Conclusion}
